#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148527} :activity/id #uuid "6cfee12a-1cb0-5520-abc8-68505a88af79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049657 :topic/id #uuid "08c0f60c-daa6-535e-8559-0b890fd64487" :topic/math-academy-id 2472} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148687 :tutorial/content "To model an exponential decay situation, it is often useful to start by setting up a function which represents the exponential decay. Then, we can evaluate that function as needed.\n\nFor example, suppose that a car's value depreciates by one-fifth every year. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]25\\,000,$ then what function gives the car's value after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, note that if the car's value reduces by $\\dfrac 1 5$ every year, then $\\dfrac 4 5$ of its value remains every year.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 25\\,000$ is the **initial value** of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 4 5$ is the **decay factor**.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the car's value after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 25\\,000\\left(\\dfrac 4 5\\right)^t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of the car after $5$ years, we substitute $t=5$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(5)=25\\,000\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{5}=8\\,192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth $\\[/math] 8\\,192$ in $5$ years." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc222367-f615-54c3-85ae-9459900ad1cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ea762fc0-a1cb-53ed-ba8b-e09b7fb84745" :step/math-academy-id 7114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147207 :question/id #uuid "34889441-479d-59e6-b9c6-a3972ff9ea60" :question/math-academy-id "e-2068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64181d45-ac95-5c6c-86bb-407a0a3cb811" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558525}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Rate"} :step/id #uuid "901a35f0-83a6-5c46-ac56-6eb267f4959f" :step/math-academy-id 7115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147571 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147208 :question/id #uuid "06d40324-f901-5e87-93c1-318a819eb635" :question/math-academy-id "e-2070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "873b588f-41f6-561a-99bf-f6764a9338ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050339}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558533}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Factor"} :step/id #uuid "a23a1883-2c0b-531a-a8b0-5f58e4ea4a0a" :step/math-academy-id 7117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148688 :tutorial/content "It's common to use a **decay rate** (rather than a decay factor) when describing exponential decay. A decay rate is typically expressed using a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that after treatment with a particular antibiotic, the number of infected cells in a specimen decreased by $30\\%$ per hour. If there were approximately $5$ million infected cells at the beginning of the treatment, how many infected cells remained after $3$ hours?\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the number of infected cells after $t$ hours. The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1-r)^t.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is very similar to the formula for exponential growth. The difference here is that we have a minus $(-)$ instead of a plus $(+)$ in the parentheses.\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of infected cells (in millions).\n\n- $r = 0.30$ is the decay rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny  = 5(1-0.3)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above gives\n\n$$\ny(t) =5(0.7)^{t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function for $t=3$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(3) = 5(0.7)^{3} = 1.715.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ hours, there were $1.715$ million infected cells.\n\nWhen writing down a formula for exponential decay, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If we're given a **decay rate** (usually expressed as a percent), then we use the formula $y(t) = a(1-r)^t.$\n\n- If we're given a **decay factor**, then we use the formula $y(t) = ab^t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eac27d5f-01b7-53c0-a804-7d42daa3f1e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1969 :tutorial/title "Modeling Exponential Decay Using a Decay Rate"} :step/id #uuid "21e9116a-98fc-52ac-a3d4-5c0ce2837688" :step/math-academy-id 7123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148528}}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Decay With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147672}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558659} :activity/id #uuid "d37c9ba1-7a39-5831-87ff-34c70ac9d8df" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049657 :topic/id #uuid "08c0f60c-daa6-535e-8559-0b890fd64487" :topic/math-academy-id 2472} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5f1225-6f0a-505c-86ce-562e015af918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (0.6)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557894} {:answer/id #uuid "2de2448f-732d-557d-b173-162765b2dd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (0.94)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557962} {:answer/id #uuid "88841433-8f87-5d36-9b04-98fc315ada9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (0.96)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558135} {:answer/id #uuid "9c041606-fc36-5db6-8431-7d0c0feaf2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (0.4)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558174} {:answer/id #uuid "c09e46e2-3be6-5701-9069-54411490d90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (0.06)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b826ca2-dd9d-5e40-a2b1-27a1b445aeb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "476bf309-f448-501a-8dca-89cb923e9fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-62052" :question/problem "Austin's average monthly profit has been decreasing at a rate of $6%$ every year. If he initially recorded an average monthly profit of $$58\\,000$, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ Austin's average monthly profit since the start of the decline?" :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=58\\,000$ is the initial average monthly profit.\n\n- $r=0.06$ is the decay rate ($6%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (1-0.06)^{t}=58\\,000\\cdot (0.94)^{t}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f1bf5b6-2615-592e-aedc-3e9f1565ab81" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1780eced-9ab1-5f96-8157-861bdec2da92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557921} {:answer/id #uuid "22bd5fa6-46e3-5592-96b9-b09472d6e659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557940} {:answer/id #uuid "acc98af6-0c0d-5c03-9cc8-ce22b28bc5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558209} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20887a-51b2-57bb-8eac-b5c6d40d238c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558301} {:answer/id #uuid "f49c2365-4c22-5026-b8ab-7656932789ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558301} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80b6de0-d629-5831-8a5c-74da1f4dac9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85b49133-7772-5722-8e3e-dbe769864a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-62061" :question/problem "A lake currently has a depth of $320$ feet. The depth of the lake is expected to decrease by one-tenth every year.\n\nThe function $d(t)$ models the depth of the lake after $t$ years and is given by\n\n$$\nd(t)=320\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" :question/worked-solution "If the depth reduces by $\\frac{1}{10}$ every month, then $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the depth remains every year. So the decay factor is $\\frac{9}{10}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nd(t)=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=320$ is the initial depth.\n\n- $b=\\frac{9}{10}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $d(t)$ is\n\n$$\nd(t)=320\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{10})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{9}{10}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4812dcec-6a52-59bd-bb5b-c5e1a060a776"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adaf691-aa9c-5bc6-b97f-4fecc09a46b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ mg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557959} {:answer/id #uuid "31d63a7a-9931-5043-95f8-51d42fe3cd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$13$ mg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557969} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbb1c4d-d71a-536c-aae7-34b9dda5e56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ mg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558110} {:answer/id #uuid "dd65015e-d881-5c60-ad4b-103340e29f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ mg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558304} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e9b59d-2751-5ad3-b00f-47582f8eab5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ mg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558323} :answer-field/id #uuid "412474d7-c770-5c0d-8c2c-215a6bc5fac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6c8488d4-9154-55d8-8e4c-cbf91ba1e38c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1418" :question/problem "An adult drinks a cup of coffee that contains $110$ mg of caffeine, and each hour the amount of caffeine in their body decreases by $15%.$ To the nearest integer, how much caffeine will be left in their system after $11$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=110$ is the initial amount of caffeine in the adult's body.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the decay rate ($15%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =110\\cdot (1-0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =110\\cdot (0.85)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for $t=11$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(11)=110\\cdot (0.85)^{11}≈18.\n$$\n\nHence, after $11$ hours, there will be $18$ mg of caffeine left in their system."} :step/id #uuid "9459a6e5-4631-59b7-af40-bc1c6ff0d9df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558605}}] :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Decay With Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557867}]}
